Reliability and a trusted baseline first, then honest checks and the fuse profile, then fixed gate classes, then speed and always-a-solid. What follows may be reordered once the profile shows where the time and failures really are. The 2.0 numbering avoids the old 1.0.7 to 1.3.0 tags on GitHub.
Rules for every release
1.
If a release misses its done line, stop and replan before the next one starts.
2.
Each release ships as a lossless Windows zip and a source zip, with release notes; test builds install as MeshSolid Test Net alongside the working install.
3.
Runs are deterministic, so every run records its STEP hash and the scoreboard compares hashes automatically. A second run is needed for one part per release, and for any part whose hash changes without a code reason.
4.
Figures are never compared across spec versions; any change to exclusion or measurement rules bumps the spec version.
5.
The scoreboard must not get worse on any part without a written reason.
6.
Measure before redesigning: no rewrite of a stage starts without a profile or failure table that shows the cause.
7.
A new path replaces an old one only behind a flag, and only after it beats the old path on the test set.
8.
Every feature is kept only if it lowers the deviation; a build that can't meet the rules fails with a reason or ships as _APPROX, never a fake pass.
9.
Gate class numbers are fixed constants, never recomputed per run from a mesh.
10.
KK&K is now a development part, because its results have been looked at too often. Holdouts are fresh scans, one per part family, never tuned against and run once at the end of a release.
11.
Reports show capped and uncapped rms, scan noise, missing material, balance cuts and other exclusions on separate lines, spec version, run time, engine version, run ID and commit.
12.
Standing rules stay: balance cuts excluded within their own cap, Hirth teeth modelled, 2 mm auto-exclusion with overrides, smooth vanes, Python 3.10 left as the default.
13.
Coding agents work on branches with tests that really run. Branch protection on main in GitHub (no force-push, pull requests required) enforces it, and is turned on before 2.0.0 starts.
