2.0.1 Honest checks (spec v3)
1.
One exclusion list shared by the engine and Metrology (they disagreed on 6.76% vs 4.20% excluded and 0 vs 4 balance cuts). Balance cuts get their own cap on depth and total area, are reported on their own line beside the other-exclusions figure, and count against their own cap rather than the 5% one, so a chipped area can't be hidden as a balance cut.
2.
Hand-entered features are handled honestly: the volume check subtracts the design bore, and the solid-to-scan support check ignores it. 'Valid solid' is defined as exactly one closed solid that passes the kernel check, with the solid count always logged. Each mesh's source (scan or CAD export) is set by you per part in parts.csv. The engine's own classification is only a cross-check that warns when it disagrees, and it never sets a gate. (It labelled Stage 3, a real scan, as a CAD tessellation at 0.011 mm noise and set a 0.009 mm gate from that. Decimated scans, about 130k faces from 300k–1.5M, have exact vertices and fool the classifier.)
3.
Day one, before any rule changes: re-measure the 2.0.0 baseline solids under spec v3, so v2 and v3 figures exist for the same solids and every change can be traced to a documented rule.
4.
Fuse profile (measurement only): time each boolean in the Stage 3 build to find where the 1,112 s goes (pairwise fuses, one multi-argument boolean, or the vane surfaces), and try OpenCascade's parallel and glue options on the slowest one. If 2.0.1 runs long, the fuse profile splits into its own release.
Done when
Stage 3 gives the same solid; Metrology changes only by the documented rules, shown by the like-for-like v2 and v3 measurement of the 2.0.0 baseline solids, and is logged as spec v3; volume is within 1% once the bore is accounted for; the support check no longer fails on the bore; and a timing table shows where the fuse time goes.
