2.0.2 Test parts and gate classes
1.
A demo-part generator in the 3DSRE style: noisy, tilted scans of a thin-walled housing with bosses, a domed cover, a lever, a pulley with a V-groove, a spur gear, a bent sheet bracket, a casting with drafts and a hex bolt, plus hundreds of random variants and a missing-material percentage in every report.
2.
Scan noise measured from repeat scans of an impeller and of a turned or prismatic part, not from each mesh, because the estimator read facet size as noise on Stage 3. The scan setup (scanner, spray or not, part size) is recorded with each part, and the class constants are tied to it.
3.
Noise budget for the 0.03 mm target (spec v2 figures, provisional): with noise about 0.013 mm, Stage 3's 0.0449 mm capped rms means a model error of about 0.043 mm, so the target needs about 0.027 mm (roughly a 60% cut in error variance), or about 0.025 mm if noise is 0.017 mm. Spec v3 excludes the 255° balance cut, which will lower the 0.0449 mm, so the model error and target are recomputed here from the v3 baseline and the repeat-scan noise, and labelled v3.
4.
Gate acceptance changes here, not earlier: once the class constants are signed off, a solid outside its class limit is rejected (or later ships as _APPROX), and no secondary rule such as blade scatter can override the class.
5.
Gate classes (machined, printed, organic, rough scan) set once from that figure as fixed constants you sign off. They are never recomputed per run; 0.009 mm machined is retired.
Done when
One command scores every demo part and names the failing stage, the repeat-scan noise figures for both parts are recorded and the model-error target for 0.03 mm is worked out from them, and the class constants are signed off and in the code.
Tested on
Box at low resolution; the two repeat scans from you.
Built by
Me: generator on standard effort; noise figure and gate classes on high effort.
2.0.3 Faster fuse and always a solid
1.
Fuse fix chosen from the 2.0.1 profile: parallel or glue booleans, one multi-argument fuse, simpler vane surfaces, or combining copies where pieces really don't touch.
2.
Split and keep: surfaces cut each other, pieces that lie on the scan are kept, then sewn into a solid. local_trim stays behind a flag and remains the default until the new path beats it on the test set.
3.
Always give something: if every route fails, a freeform fallback writes a valid solid STEP named with an _APPROX suffix, with the verdict LOW ACCURACY (never PASS), the approximate areas marked in the report, and a visible warning when the add-in imports it.
Done when
Stage 3 total time under 5 minutes (was 1,299 s) with Metrology within ±1% of the 2.0.1 baseline; the five shell-failure jobs each give one valid solid; and no demo or held-out part ends without a STEP.
Tested on
Demo parts on the box; Stage 3 on the PC, with a second run only per the STEP-hash rule.
